This came from a live session where the bot presented conflicts grouped
by component rather than diff order. The user asked "can you talk about
the conflicts in the order of the diff?" The rule was saved to a
project-level memory file at the time, but belongs in the skill so it
applies universally.

The placement in §4 (after git diff --check, before the build gate) is
where the AUTO_MERGE diff is already introduced. Keeping the
presentation-order rule adjacent to the verification commands it
applies to felt more natural than a separate §4.5.

Unrelated tip: git supports a diff order file (diff.orderFile config,
or git diff -O <file>) that controls the order files appear in diff
output. For projects with a natural reading order — say, headers before
sources, proto definitions before generated code, config before
implementation — setting this up once makes every diff output easier
to scan. Not something for the skill, but worth knowing if the default
diff order is consistently unhelpful on a project.
